    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Franco-British counter-attack of September 1914 halted the German advance on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan">Schlieffen Plan</a>, the German plan to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in about six weeks.</li><li>A gap of 50 km opened between the German 1st and 2nd Armies, and the French 5th Army and the British Expeditionary Force advanced into it, while communications between the German armies and Moltke's headquarters were poor.</li><li>Casualty estimates differ widely: about 250,000 French, 12,733 British and 298,000 Germans in one estimate, and about 300,000 for all sides in another that questions whether the battle can be separated from its surroundings.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gallieni">Joseph Gallieni</a> commandeered about 600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> taxicabs on the night of 7 to 8 September to carry soldiers to the front; the meters ran, and the French treasury paid a fare of 70,012 francs.</p></aside>
    </section>

