    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">August 1914</span><span class="rf-text">German armies win most of the battles of the opening campaign and overrun much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late August 1914</span><span class="rf-text">Joffre abandons Plan XVII and the Great Retreat of the French and British begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early September 1914</span><span class="rf-text">Moltke changes the German plan, and Kluck's 1st Army turns south-east and crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Early September 1914</span><span class="rf-text">Joffre halts the retreat and orders a counter-offensive</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1914</span><span class="rf-text">The 6th Army meets the German IV Reserve Corps at the Ourcq, and the 5th Army and the BEF advance into the gap between the 1st and 2nd Armies</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1914</span><span class="rf-text">Hentsch's staff tour leads to a general German retreat to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1914</span><span class="rf-text">Moltke is replaced by Falkenhayn, and the First Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a> begins</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September to October 1914</span><span class="rf-text">Both sides try to turn each other's northern flank in the Race to the Sea</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="first-battle-of-the-marne" data-v="1de417ba57" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The 6th Army meets the German right flank</b> French reservists of the 6th Army, probing northeast of Paris, met the German IV Reserve Corps near the Ourcq. Though outnumbered, the corps attacked and held them for 24 hours before it retreated.</li><li><b>Kluck turns west and the 5th Army attacks Bülow</b> Kluck turned his army to face west against the 6th Army and moved off its attacks. The right wing of the 5th Army attacked the German 2nd Army and pinned it, while the turn left a gap between the two German armies.</li><li><b>Bülow's right wing falls back</b> After attacks by the French 5th Army, Bülow ordered his right wing to retreat 15 km. The 2nd Army, worn and depleted, went on the defensive south of the Marne.</li><li><b>Hausen attacks Foch in the marshes at night</b> With Bülow's right retreating, his left wing attacked with Hausen's 3rd Army. Hausen struck Foch's 9th Army at night and pushed it back 13 km, and then the attack bogged down, its soldiers exhausted.</li><li><b>The BEF and the 5th Army enter the gap</b> The Allies sent the BEF northwest toward Kluck and the 5th Army northeast toward Bülow, into the gap between the two German armies. The BEF crossed the Petit Morin and established a bridgehead over the Marne.</li><li><b>Quast breaks through the 6th Army</b> Kluck had telegraphed that an enveloping attack by General von Quast would bring the decision. Quast fought his way through the 6th Army's defences, and the way to Paris, 50 km away, lay open.</li><li><b>Hentsch and Bülow agree on a general retreat</b> Hentsch reached the 2nd Army headquarters and found Bülow's right flank near collapse. Bülow ordered a 20 km withdrawal, and Hentsch agreed that a general retreat would be needed once the Allies crossed the Marne.</li><li><b>Hentsch reaches Kluck and the 1st Army follows</b> Bülow ordered another withdrawal on the morning of 9 September. Hentsch reached Kluck's headquarters at 11:30 and said a general retreat was necessary, and Kluck, with Bülow retreating, had no choice but to follow.</li><li><b>Moltke orders the 3rd, 4th and 5th Armies back</b> Moltke visited the 3rd, 4th and 5th Armies and ordered them to retreat to the Aisne, in addition to the retreat of the 1st and 2nd Armies already under way.</li><li><b>The Germans dig in north of the Aisne</b> The Germans stopped retreating after 65 km and dug in north of the Aisne. The French and British followed, though the exhausted Allied armies averaged only 19 km a day.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gallieni"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-gallieni.jpg" alt="Joseph Gallieni" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gallieni">Joseph Gallieni</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gen._Gallieni_LCCN2014697689_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bain News Service, publisher, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 3 August 1914, and both sides expected a short war. Germany faced a war on two fronts, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the east and against France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the west. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan">Schlieffen Plan</a>, as revised by Helmuth von Moltke, the German armies were to sweep through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and into France, capture <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and envelop and destroy the French army east of the city within about six weeks, and then turn on Russia. The violation of Belgian neutrality brought the United Kingdom into the war, and Britain sent the British Expeditionary Force (BEF) to France. France's chief aim was to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>, lost to Germany in 1870, and its Plan XVII expected the main German force in the east.</p><p>August brought bloody battles that the Germans nearly all won. Charles Lanrezac and the retired general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-gallieni">Joseph Gallieni</a> warned Joffre that the main German effort lay in the north-west, and on 24 August Joffre acknowledged that his left flank was at risk. He abandoned Plan XVII, and the French 3rd, 4th and 5th Armies and the BEF began a retreat of 300 km, mostly walking rather than fighting. Joffre created the 6th Army to defend Paris and the 9th Army under Ferdinand Foch, and moved troops by rail from his eastern armies.</p><p>The pursuing Germans had outrun their supplies. Kluck's 1st Army was 140 km beyond its railhead, and sixty per cent of its motor transport had broken down. Moltke had also reduced the force in France by 200,000 or more men, sending two corps east against the Russians and two to besiege <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maubeuge">Maubeuge</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had seven armies on the western front, and three on its right flank were most involved at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>: the 1st Army under Alexander von Kluck, which began the war with 320,000 men, the 2nd under Karl von Bülow with 260,000, and the 3rd under Max von Hausen with 180,000. These numbers had fallen by September. The 1st and 2nd Armies had 580,000 soldiers on 1 August and 372,000 at the battle. On the French side the 4th Army under Fernand de Langle de Cary, the 5th Army, the new 6th Army under Michel-Joseph Maunoury and Foch's 9th Army were most involved. Joffre dismissed Lanrezac on 3 September and replaced him with Louis Franchet d'Espèrey. The BEF, under John French, numbered 130,000 at the start of the war.</p><p>On the eve of battle the allied forces between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> numbered 980,000 French and 100,000 British soldiers, against 750,000 Germans. The Germans had 3,300 guns to 3,000.</p><p>The commanders differed. Moltke stayed at his headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and gave his army commanders wide latitude. Joffre visited his armies often and dismissed more than 30 French generals. John French, shaken by the early British casualties, had been reluctant to engage the Germans. On 2 September Moltke ordered Paris bypassed on the north and made Bülow's army the main striking force, with Kluck to follow in echelon. Kluck instead turned south-east, crossed the Marne on 3 September and pursued the French 5th Army, unaware of the new 6th Army guarding Paris on his right.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On 4 September Joffre ordered the retreat halted and a counter-offensive to begin on 6 September. On 5 September part of Maunoury's 6th Army, probing north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> near the Ourcq, met the German IV Reserve Corps of 24,000 men under Hans von Gronau. Gronau attacked and held the French for 24 hours, which warned Kluck, who turned his army west and marched 130 km in two days to face the 6th Army. On the night of 7 to 8 September Gallieni sent soldiers to the front by rail, truck and about 600 Paris taxis. On 9 September Kluck's general von Quast broke through the 6th Army's defences, and the road to Paris, 50 km away, was open.</p><p>Meanwhile Bülow's depleted 2nd Army was attacked by the French 5th Army, and Hausen assaulted Foch's 9th Army at night on 8 September, pushing it back 13 km before his attack bogged down. Kluck's turn had opened a 50 km gap between his army and Bülow's, which French aerial reconnaissance discovered. The BEF and the 5th Army advanced into it, though the British moved slowly, advancing only 40 km in three days against a force they outnumbered ten to one.</p><p>On 8 September Moltke's staff officer Richard Hentsch visited the armies. At Bülow's headquarters he agreed that a general retreat would be necessary if the French and British crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>, and on 9 September he told Kluck's staff that a retreat was necessary. Bülow and Kluck withdrew, and on 11 September Moltke ordered the other armies back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;First Battle of the Marne&#8221; cites where it covers The combatants, The eve of battle, The battle, Kluck:</p><ul><li>John Keegan, The First World war (Alfred A. Knopf, 1998)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German armies retreated about 65 km to the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>, where they dug in on the heights. The Franco-British pursuit averaged only 19 km a day. The Germans lost 11,717 prisoners, 30 field guns and 100 machine guns to the French and 3,500 prisoners to the British. Moltke wrote to his wife on the night of 9 September that things had not gone well. On 14 September he was told to resign for ill health, and Erich von Falkenhayn replaced him. The retreat ended the German hope of pushing the French beyond the Verdun-Marne-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> line and winning a quick victory.</p><p>Casualties are hard to separate from those of the other battles of August and September. One estimate gives about 250,000 French, 12,733 British and 298,000 German casualties. Herwig estimated about 300,000 for all sides, with 67,700 for the five German armies, about 85,000 French and 1,701 British. Nelipovich estimated German losses at 10,602 killed, 16,815 missing and 47,432 wounded, and Entente losses at about 268,500.</p><p>The French 6th Army and the BEF advanced for four days against rearguards, but could not catch the Germans, and frontal attacks on the Aisne on 15 and 16 September were repulsed. Both sides then tried to turn each other's northern flank in the Race to the Sea, which led to the First Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a> and to trench warfare along a front that stayed nearly stable for four years.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;First Battle of the Marne&#8221; cites where it covers The Great Retreat, Aftermath, Casualties, Subsequent operations:</p><ul><li>J. E. Edmonds, Military Operations France and Belgium 1914: Mons, the Retreat to the Seine, the Marne and the Aisne August–October 1914 (Macmillan, 1926)</li><li>I. Sumner, The First Battle of the Marne 1914: The French 'miracle' halts the Germans (Osprey, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Historians reconstruct the battle from the memoirs and orders of its commanders and from the armies' official histories. Gallieni and Joffre wrote memoirs, and Moltke wrote to his wife on the night of 9 September. French casualty figures come from the French official history, Les armées françaises dans la grande guerre, German figures from the Sanitätsberichte and British figures from the British Official History. Later historians include John Keegan, Holger Herwig, Barbara Tuchman, Strachan and Ian Sumner.</p><p>Several points are disputed. Moltke is said to have told the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a>, "Your Majesty, we have lost the war", but whether he said so is not known. Moltke's instructions to Hentsch were verbal and not written, so what authority Hentsch held to order a retreat is a matter of what the participants reported. Gallieni wrote that some had exaggerated the importance of the taxis, and Strachan and Herwig regard their military effect as slight, though the effect on French morale is undeniable. Meyer wrote that the battle was "settled by one side's decision not to fight".</p><p>The casualty figures are uncertain, since over two million men fought in the campaign and no exact official counts exist. Estimates of casualties along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> front for all armies are often c. 500,000 killed or wounded, against Herwig's 300,000, and Herwig himself questioned whether isolating the battle was justified.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;First Battle of the Marne&#8221; cites where it covers Analysis:</p><ul><li>I. Sumner, The First Battle of the Marne 1914: The French 'miracle' halts the Germans (Osprey, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German retreat marked the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schlieffen-plan">Schlieffen Plan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had hoped to win quickly in the west, and the failure meant a long war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, whose combined resources were far greater than its own. Richard Brooks wrote that Joffre, by frustrating the plan, had won the decisive battle of the war and perhaps of the century. Herwig called it the most important land battle of the twentieth century, and John J. Tierney Jr argued that it was the most important battle in history. It was also one of the first battles in which reconnaissance aircraft played an important role, by finding the gap in the German lines.</p><p>Others judge it a partial success. John Terraine wrote that it never presented the traditional aspect of victory, yet called it the decisive battle of the war. Tuchman called it an incomplete victory, Doughty wrote that the opportunity for a decisive victory had slipped from Joffre's hands, and Ian Sumner called it a flawed victory. Joffre credited d'Espèrey with making it possible, and Kluck, according to Tuchman, put the German failure down to the French soldier's capacity to recover quickly.</p><p>In French memory it is the "miracle on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>" (miracle de la Marne). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> taxis, though militarily slight in the view of Strachan and Herwig, became the most storied event of the battle.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>From 5 to 12 September 1914 French and British armies counter-attacked the German right wing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, exploited a gap between two German armies and forced a retreat to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>. The German plan for a quick victory failed, and the western front settled into trenches.</em></p>

